An application of a lambda or of a function value whose parameter or result is an `ORDINAL` now reduces to the body at the argument, as it does for other types, so `((p: ORDINAL) => p)(o) == o`, `((x: int) => o)(0) == o` and `((p: ORDINAL) => if p == o then 1 else 0)(o) == 1` verify (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/363). The verifier represents an `ORDINAL` by a value of its type of boxes without boxing it, and it left an `ORDINAL` argument, lambda body or function result unboxed where an application expects a boxed value. A `forall` statement that assigns an `ORDINAL` field, such as `forall c | c in s { c.f := o + 1; }`, now gives the field the assigned value; it boxed some right-hand sides. No axiom or assumption is added; the translation of other types is unchanged.
